# duty.claim — <statement-kind> <check that must be called in that arm>
#
# ⚠ THIS FILE EXISTS BECAUSE A CLAIM-FREE RULE PROVABLY CANNOT DO THIS, and the
# proof is a measurement, not an opinion. Commit 756aed65 added the missing call
# `field_borrow_conflicts` to visit_stmt's Assign arm. Asked on the revision
# BEFORE it, over the statement domain:
#
#     before:  NO statement arm calls field_borrow_conflicts anywhere.  Zero.
#     after:   exactly one — visit_stmt / Assign.
#
# So "the siblings of one switch are their own specification" — the premise
# arm_divergence was built on — cannot reach this defect: before the fix there
# was no sibling doing the right thing to learn from. The knowledge that an
# ASSIGNMENT is a write, and that a write owes the conflict check, lives nowhere
# in the code until someone writes the call. It has to be asserted.
#
# ⚠ AND THE DOMINANCE RULE MISSED IT FOR A SECOND, DUMBER REASON, recorded here
# so the next rule does not repeat it: dominance.dl selected candidate checks
# with `sites >= 2` in the same function. visit_stmt called the check ZERO times
# before the fix and ONE after — so the selector excluded exactly the case under
# test, on both sides, and printed byte-identical output for a tree with the
# defect and a tree without it. That is the SAME error as `walker(F) :- handles
# >= 2 projections`, which lost try_path at 1/5. NEVER LET THE SELECTOR AND THE
# PREDICATE SHARE A QUANTITY: a function that is missing the check is, by
# definition, one that calls it seldom.
#
# The claim is small, visible and reviewable — which is the point. An assertion
# in a file can be argued with; the same assertion buried in a rule's threshold
# cannot.

# A WRITE to a place owes the field-borrow conflict check: writing through a
# place must not be admitted while an overlapping path is borrowed. Every
# spelling of a write, because the property is "this stores through a place"
# and the property does not care how the store is spelled.
Assign           field_borrow_conflicts
FieldWrite       field_borrow_conflicts
IndexWrite       field_borrow_conflicts
FieldIndexWrite  field_borrow_conflicts
DerefWrite       field_borrow_conflicts
DerefFieldWrite  field_borrow_conflicts
TupleWrite       field_borrow_conflicts
ChainFieldWrite  field_borrow_conflicts
